# Fred Memory Complex V1 (MEM-01)

MEM-01 is the first executable memory milestone for Fred. It stores immutable
Experience evidence and derived Memory interpretations in one SurrealDB
cognitive substrate, retrieves them through vector, BM25, structured/entity,
and graph channels, and consolidates Memory through the bounded Sleep cycle.

The implementation follows these core invariants:

- Experience is immutable after commit.
- Memory starts `PROVISIONAL` and normal retrieval sees only `ACTIVE` Memory.
- A proposer and an independent evidence-verifier are separate model calls.
- Model output never writes directly to persistence.
- Sleep operations are exactly `KEEP`, `MERGE`, `PROMOTE`, and `TOMBSTONE`.
- Sleep may mutate Memory but never Experience.
- The Observer is read-only with respect to cognitive state.

## PowerShell setup

From `C:\Users\nolan\AIProjects\fred`:

```powershell
python -m venv .venv
.\.venv\Scripts\Activate.ps1
python -m pip install -e ".[dev]"
```

Fred reads configuration from process environment variables and otherwise uses
the defaults in `FredConfig`. The repository includes `.env.example` as a
reference for variable names; copying it to `.env` does not load it
automatically.

The default runtime expects SurrealDB 3.3.x. `compose.yaml` pins
`surrealdb/surrealdb:v3.3.0`.

```powershell
docker compose up -d
```

The default cognitive and embedding models are intentionally fixed for the
first qualification cycle:

```powershell
ollama pull qwen3:8b
ollama pull qwen3-embedding:0.6b
```

Do not silently substitute another embedding model during MEM-01 acceptance.

## Health and schema

```powershell
fred health
fred migrate
```

`fred health` exits non-zero unless SurrealDB, Ollama, `qwen3:8b`, and
`qwen3-embedding:0.6b` are all available.

## Deterministic verification

```powershell
python -m compileall src tests
ruff check .
pytest -q
```

The deterministic suite uses an in-memory backend and stub models. It does not
count as live SurrealDB/Ollama experiment acceptance.

## Memory Observer

The Observer reads the same persisted substrate and traces without writing
cognitive state:

```powershell
fred observer
fred observer --session-id <session-id>
```

It exposes Experience, State, Memory lifecycle state, retrieval traces,
foreground proposal/validation traces, Sleep traces, provenance, and errors as
machine-readable JSON.

## Behavioural experiments

Each live run uses an isolated SurrealDB database and writes the required
artifacts under `artifacts/memory_v1/<experiment>/<run-id>/`:

```text
run.json
trace.jsonl
metrics.json
result.json
```

Run one frozen experiment with the required five independent runs:

```powershell
fred experiment EXP-01 --runs 5
fred experiment EXP-02 --runs 5
fred experiment EXP-03 --runs 5
fred experiment EXP-04 --runs 5
```

Or run the full behavioural acceptance set:

```powershell
fred experiment all --runs 5
```

The acquisition and probe phases use different stateless Ollama model wrapper
instances while retaining the same persisted database. EXP-03's hidden
`X/Y/Z` mapping and EXP-04's hidden rule remain harness-only; the model receives
only task inputs plus `SUCCESS`/`FAILURE` feedback.

## Guarded experiment cleanup

Every experiment database name is generated in the form
`fred_mem01_exp0N_<12 hex chars>`. The cleanup command refuses any other name:

```powershell
fred reset-experiment-db fred_mem01_exp01_0123456789ab
```

The normal `fred_mem01` database is therefore outside the accepted reset
pattern.

## Research boundaries

MEM-01 deliberately excludes Temporal Guidance, a Digital Cerebellum,
age-based decay, archival/pruning subsystems, distributed services, and UI
polish. The research source of truth is `docs/MEMORY_COMPLEX_V1_SPEC.md`, with
the build, Python, experiment, failure-map, and SurrealDB contracts alongside
it in this repository.
